---
title: Ardeidae
---
# Ardeidae

Ardeidae is the heron, egret and bittern family, long-legged wading birds that
catch fish in shallow water. A heron hunts by standing still at the margin and
striking down as a fish comes within reach, so the catch is made from above the
surface rather than beneath it. The neck folds into an S at rest and straightens
explosively in the strike, powered by a kinked sixth vertebra unique to the
family. Most species catch fish, frogs and insects by day, though the night
herons are named for the hours they hunt in. See [[grey-heron]].
